Title: A Christmas Carol  
Credit: Written by  
Author: Charles Dickens  
Source: Adapted from book  
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE’S COUNTING HOUSE – CHRISTMAS EVE

Scrooge, a thin, dour man in a shabby suit, sits at a small desk piled with ledgers. The fire is small and pale. His clerk, BOB CRATCHIT, works at a cramped cell in the background. A cheerful knock sounds at the door.

  
SCROOGE

Bah! Humbug!

  
BOB CRATCHIT  
(quietly, timidly)  
Merry Christmas, sir…

  
The door opens and NEPHEW FRED enters in bright clothing.

  
FRED

Christmas is a time for warmth and cheer, Uncle! Come, join us for dinner.

  
SCROOGE  
(sharply)  
I keep Christmas in my own way, and you shall have no company in mine.

  
Bob exchanges a worried look as Fred departs. Scrooge turns back to his work with an indifferent scowl.

  
  
EXT. SCROOGE’S HOME – NIGHT

The foggy London street lies silent and looming. Scrooge walks home alone from the counting-house. He passes an ancient warehouse door under which a flicker of ghostly light shows a shape upon the knocker.

  
SCROOGE (V.O.)  
Old Marley was dead as a door-nail… yet something stirs on this bitter night.

  
Scrooge shudders as he touches the knocker. In that moment, the knocker seems to morph into a spectral face.

  
  
INT. SCROOGE’S BEDROOM – LATE NIGHT

In a dimly lit chamber, Scrooge sits by a nearly extinguished fire. The door opens with a deep, echoing sound. MARLEY’S GHOST, bound in chains of old ledgers and keys, enters silently.

  
MARLEY’S GHOST  
(ominously)  
I am here to warn you, Ebenezer Scrooge. I wear the chain I forged in life, and you, too, will be haunted by Three Spirits.

  
Scrooge recoils, his face a mixture of shock and disbelief.

  
SCROOGE  
(voice trembling)  
Ghost? I—I don’t believe in such things!

  
MARLEY’S GHOST  
Their visits shall confer upon you a chance to amend your ways. Heed my words… for in this night, your fate is sealed.

  
The ghost’s chains clank as he retreats through the door. Scrooge, heart pounding, slumps into his chair as the room grows silent.

  
  
INT. MEMORY – A YOUNGER SCROOGE’S STUDY – FLASHBACK

A soft, luminous light spills over a modest study. A young SCROOGE, with a spark of hope in his eyes, pores over a book. Enter the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST, a gentle, ethereal figure in a simple white robe with a holly wreath on its head.

  
GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST  
(softly)  
I am the Spirit of Christmas Past. Come, let us revisit the moments when your heart was light.

  
Young Scrooge gazes up in wonder as images of childhood merriment, a joyful apprenticeship, and festive gatherings shimmer in the air in spectral vignettes.

  
YOUNG SCROOGE  
(smiling wistfully)  
Once, I believed in the wonder of Christmas…

  
The Spirit’s hand gestures, and the scenes fade into soft memory as the young man’s laughter mingles with distant carols.

  
  
EXT. CRATCHIT HOUSE – CHRISTMAS MORNING

A humble home glows warmly despite its modest means. Inside, the CRATCHIT FAMILY gathers around a cheerful, if meager, table. BOB CRATCHIT sits with a hopeful smile. Tiny TIM, frail but joyful, raises his eyes.

  
GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT (V.O.)  
Witness the warmth of a poor family’s love, untouched by the chill of greed.

  
Tiny Tim, with gentle innocence, proclaims:

  
TINY TIM  
(smiling weakly)  
God bless us, every one!

  
The family exchanges tender glances as laughter and hope fill the small room.

  
  
EXT. LONDON ALLEY – NIGHT (FUTURE VISION)

In a cold, desolate graveyard under a starless sky, a solitary, neglected tomb stands. A dark and silent figure, the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, cloaked in deep black, approaches slowly. Scrooge, trembling, follows at a distance.

  
SCROOGE  
(hoarse whisper)  
Show me… what fate awaits me. Is this the end that awaits my soul?

  
The Spirit gestures toward a weathered tombstone. Scrooge’s eyes widen as he reads in a trembling voice:

  
SCROOGE  
(agonized)  
“EBENEZER SCROOGE”... Is that truly me?

  
Silence falls heavy as the ghost’s presence deepens the foreboding atmosphere. Scrooge falls to his knees, overcome by dread and regret.

  
SCROOGE (V.O.)  
If these shadows remain unaltered by the future, must I be condemned to a fate of loneliness and despair?

  
  
INT. SCROOGE’S BEDROOM – CHRISTMAS MORNING

Sunlight floods the modest room. Scrooge awakens suddenly, gasping and overcome with relief. His eyes shine with tears of repentance and hope as he clutches his bed-curtain.

  
SCROOGE  
(breathlessly)  
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits have shown me the error of my ways. I am a changed man!

  
He rises and hurriedly dresses, determination replacing his old bitterness.

  
  
EXT. LONDON STREETS – CHRISTMAS MORNING

A transformed Scrooge strides along the busy street with genuine warmth. He greets cheerful passersby; his step light and his countenance kind. A young boy stops him.

  
SCROOGE  
(cheerfully)  
Merry Christmas, my boy! What day is it?

  
BOY  
(grinning)  
Christmas Day, sir!

  
Scrooge smiles broadly and calls to a cab.

  
SCROOGE  
I must send a message of goodwill. Go fetch the poulterer’s man—I shall buy the largest turkey for a poor family!

  
The cab drives off. Scrooge’s eyes sparkle with newfound joy as he laughs with genuine mirth.

  
  
INT. CRATCHIT OFFICE – LATER THAT MORNING

Bob Cratchit hesitantly enters the small office. Scrooge, now radiant in generosity, rises to meet him and claps him on the back.

  
SCROOGE  
(with heartfelt warmth)  
Bob, my dear fellow! A merry Christmas to you! Today, I raise your salary and extend my hand in friendship. I will make amends for my past cruelty.

  
BOB CRATCHIT  
(astonished, hopeful)  
Thank you, sir… Thank you so much, sir!

  
Scrooge beams as he envisions the Cratchit family’s future, mindful especially of Tiny Tim’s frail smile.

  
  
EXT. SCROOGE’S COUNTING HOUSE – DAY

Scrooge exits his building with a light heart. Groups of Londoners exchange greetings, and carolers sing in the streets. Scrooge waves cheerfully at everyone he meets.

  
SCROOGE (V.O.)  
No longer shall I shut out the joy of Christmas. I have awakened to a new life—a life of compassion, kindness, and hope.

  
He walks off into the bustling crowd, transformed and determined to embrace his newfound generosity.

  
FADE OUT / THE END
